#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078261 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8012ecda-d2f4-52de-99de-7b2ebd635661" :question/math-academy-id "e-6676" :question/problem "A loaf of bread just taken out of the oven has a temperature of $180\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$. After placing it on the table, the temperature $T(t)$ of the bread decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $25\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$, which differential equation could be used to model the bread's temperature? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - T_S)\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nT_S = 25\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - 25)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078262 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3e001a8-9294-59a4-82e6-b1122afdc72f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6677" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, of a fluorescent light bulb after turning off can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -0.25(T-23)$\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the bulb is $80\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$, calculate the temperature of the bulb after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fluorescent light bulb is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -0.25(T-23)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dT}{T - 23} &= -0.25dt \\\\\n\\int \\frac{dT}{T - 23} &= - \\int 0.25dt \\\\\n\\ln \\mid T - 23 \\mid &= -0.25t + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T - 23 > 0$ for all $t > 0$.\nNext, we solve for $T(t)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT - 23 &= e^{-0.25t + C} \\\\\nT - 23 &= e^{C}e^{-0.25t} \\\\\nT - 23 &= Ke^{-0.25t} \\\\\nT &= 23 + Ke^{-0.25t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the light bulb was $80\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$. So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=80$, and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n80 &= 23 + Ke^{0} \\\\\n80 &= 23 + K \\\\\nK &= 57\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t) = 23+57e^{-0.25t}\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the bulb after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4) = 23+57e^{-0.25\\cdot 4}\\approx 44\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078295 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d7fbff5-7db5-53e4-a17e-3fb7ac7a0069" :question/math-academy-id "e-8957" :question/problem "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0$.\n\nFrom the given options, 2 is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50)\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12$, and $a=50$. Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85b8d3c4-45c3-57c2-9403-a1b0981cf83d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-2y(y - 500), y(0) = 400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075048} {:answer/id #uuid "a0638072-af92-5e17-b6b1-6a6036e0f9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 2(y - 500), y(0) = 600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075049} {:answer/id #uuid "d5852a94-6331-5384-a173-629cde50b956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 2y(y - 500), y(0) = 600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075050} {:answer/id #uuid "26c656e9-eb6a-526d-8a67-e22c14b1c437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-2(y - 500), y(0) = 400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075051} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa4aaae-b654-5e69-b571-27ea8068bf99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-2(y - 500), y(0) = 600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5aa4aaae-b654-5e69-b571-27ea8068bf99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-2(y - 500), y(0) = 600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075052} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1233007-f334-5344-aa80-675719f92937" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fb765b0-7266-558a-8445-d5e7e8dcfb07" :question/math-academy-id "q-105890" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Inhibited decay has the form $y^{\\prime}=-k(y-a)$ with $k>0$ and $y(0)>a$. The equation $y^{\\prime}=-2(y-500)$ has equilibrium $a=500$, and $y(0)=600>500$. Indeed, its solution is $y(t)=500+100e^{-2t}$, which decreases toward $500$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86bbab44-6069-592a-a4a7-b0b0db3dcec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 80(y + 20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075060} {:answer/id #uuid "c13c00a7-95a3-5962-b866-cb920a5a6130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-80(y - 20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075061} {:answer/id #uuid "eb974e6a-510e-5517-9887-f1543ffb7ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-80y(20 - y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075062} {:answer/id #uuid "1333b2f7-f369-5e68-b5d9-35050d676786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-80(20 - y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075063} {:answer/id #uuid "537e490d-a5c5-5410-b145-61849298df4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 80y(y + 20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c13c00a7-95a3-5962-b866-cb920a5a6130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-80(y - 20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075061} :answer-field/id #uuid "7710f67b-7902-53e9-9bb6-4dac11bb3641" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0fe545c-1008-5ec6-a573-a706bc6b026b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105898" :question/problem "Given that $y > 20$ and $t > 0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The inhibited decay form is $y^{\\prime}=-k(y-a)$ with $k>0$ and $y>a$. Here $-80(y-20)$ has $k=80$ and $a=20$. For $y>20$ the derivative is negative, so the solution decreases toward $20$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7ccb7df-65a9-5aa4-b3b0-cd2df9e8f72c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt} =-k(T - 164)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076000} {:answer/id #uuid "d290b344-74d3-55ac-95ce-b50a12471d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt} =-k(T - 100)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076001} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e1c165-d1cd-53a1-ac94-3acaa07397be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt} =-k(T - 64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076002} {:answer/id #uuid "eeef6b7f-0e0c-596a-b4f1-864ef7a7f25b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt} =-64(T - 100)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076003} {:answer/id #uuid "7422c721-2d61-58a6-8a40-a9a8e6e19930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt} =-100(T - 64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6e1c165-d1cd-53a1-ac94-3acaa07397be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt} =-k(T - 64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076002} :answer-field/id #uuid "5be0d10a-eb75-5635-b8ee-f963aee60758" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58a15628-6a07-582f-a2d4-e1c7a7c6e77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37365" :question/problem "A medical doctor records the temperature of $100\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{F}$ using a thermometer. After the measurement is taken, the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $64\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{F}$, which differential equation could be used to model the thermometer reading? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling uses the difference between the thermometer temperature and the room temperature: $T-64$. Therefore $\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-64)$ with $k>0$. The initial reading gives $T(0)=100$; it does not replace the ambient temperature in the differential equation."}]}
